 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to trout\nstocking practices of the department of environmental conservation",
    "basePrintNo": "S5021",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5021-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §11-0508, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5021\n\nSPONSOR: METZGER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the environmental conservation law, in\nrelation to trout stocking practices of the department of environmental\nconservation\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo establish modern hatchery practices to ensure the health of New\nYork's cold-water fisheries.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1- Title of the bill \"Healthy Coldwater Fisheries Act\".\n\nSection 2 - Legislative findings - The findings include acknowledging\nNew York as a freshwater sport fishing destination. New York has the\nsecond highest number of anglers in the United States. Climate change\nand habitat degradation pose imminent threats to fisheries and the\ncommunities that relay on them. Current hatchery practices have not kept\npace with other northeastern states. Modernizing trout stocking prac-\ntices will decrease burdens on state hatcheries and produce trout with\ngreater survival characteristics. Therefore, the Department of Environ-\nmental Conservation must establish modernized hatchery practices.\n\nSection 3 - The environmental conservation law is amended by adding a\nnew section 11-508 entitled \"Survivor strain trout stocking program\".\nNo later than December 31', 2020, the department shall promulgate regu-\nlations that direct hatcheries in the state to develop brood stock with\nhigher thermal and survival characteristics. The regulations will\nrequire trout hatcheries to collect eggs from trout with higher thermal\ntolerances to develop trout with survival characteristics, develop\nhatchery practices to encourage greater genetic diversity and survival\ncharacteristic, and identify water bodies, that would benefit from the\ndevelopment of trout brook stocks with higher thermal tolerances. These\nregulations must be protective of native and heritage strain brook trout\npopulations.\n\nThis section also requires the department to report on trout stocking\npractices in the state. The report must contain : 1) a comparison with\nother state stocking practices in the Northeast, 2) stocking measures to\npromote trout survival in thermally marginal waters, 3) stream restora-\ntion measures to promote trout survival, 4) public outreach measures to\npromote trout survival, 5) the identification of marginal water bodies\nthat would benefit from resilient stocking practices, and 6) recommended\nregulations to promote increased fish survival and reproductive viabil-\nity.\n\nNo later than December 31, 2021 the department shall implement a pilot\nprogram in at least five water bodies for stocking \"survivor strain\"\nhatchery trout. These pilots shall be in the Hudson River watershed, St\nLawrence River watershed, Delaware River watershed, Genesee River\nwatershed, and one water body in Nassau or Suffolk county.\n\nSection 4 - Effective Date - Immediately.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nThis is a new law.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York's Ausable and Beaverkill Rivers are considered the birthplace\nof modern trout fishing. In fact, it's estimated that fisheries generate\n$2.7 billion in economic activity. However, climate change and habitat\ndegradation pose a threat to New York's trout. In particular, warming\nwaters cause increased trout mortality and marginalize habitats for\nfish.\n\nThis legislation seeks to address the challenges facing trout by direct-\ning hatcheries to breed trout with higher thermal tolerances and greater\nsurvival characteristics.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
